					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mrwrong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2039383); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2039383&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606661380" alt="MrWrong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2039383)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mrwrong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2039383); return false;" >MrWrong</a></span> <span class="odate time_1470403716 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Aug 2016 13:28</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2549090">
									

<p>While the use of the calculator is somewhat interesting, the experiments are ultimately without rhyme or reason.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Experiment SCP-2618-02<br />
Subject: D-030482-38<br />
Procedure: Typed '34' into SCP-2618 and hit activation button while pointing SCP-2618 across the hall.<br />
Result: D-030482-38 appeared in SCP-███'s holding cell, approximately 34 meters along the line SCP-2618 had been aimed. Both SCP-2618-1 and D-030482-38 were safely recovered.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Experiments like these scream of truly horrendous protocols. Since it is not the first time doing this (and they can interrogate the user for answers), why use &quot;34&quot; when there are much smaller integers to work with? Why not conduct it in a controlled area (like a desert plain)? Why not have precautions, like guards stationed near the estimated arrival point or GPS tracking system?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>#ϟ0+93K&lt; &lt;QV3KV.M3ΣΣFϟ7UF+8&quot;983KV</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You honestly can't expect the general reader to take their time to decode this. If the articles does not bother, why should the reader bother about it?</p>

